WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
Most people think creativity is a gift.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.000
It's not. It needs a system. And once

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.360
you learn that system, you start

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.920
generating great ideas yourself. You

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.065
start seeing and connecting things other

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.120
people miss.

00:00:15.065 --> 00:00:18.040
>> [music]

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.320
>> You build taste that helps you stand

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.800
out. And today, that matters more than

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.520
ever. AI can generate thousands of

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.680
options before you finish your cup of

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:32.119
coffee, but more options don't mean you

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.080
are more original. Used badly, AI will

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.800
turn you into a commodity faster than

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.200
you can think. Creativity is not some

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.960
magical [music] force. The edge you need

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.720
comes from just two things: your

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.600
originality and your taste. The two

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.640
things [music] AI can't generate for

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.000
you. So, today I'll show you how to

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.880
create with AI without losing your

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.200
humanity, your [music] edge. You'll

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.640
learn how to think more creatively,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.480
sharpen your taste, and train your mind

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:03.120
to create original ideas. So, let's get

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.480
started with the story of the strangest

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.920
band in recent years. AI has created a

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:13.360
strange new problem. It can make you

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:15.880
invisible faster than ever. In 2025, a

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.240
new band called The Velvet Sundown

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.240
became super popular for some reason.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.640
They crossed 1 million listeners on

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.200
Spotify in just over a month. They

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.360
released two albums, and their song

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.700
reached Spotify's Viral 50 chart in

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:34.440
Britain, in Norway, and Sweden. And they

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:38.080
[music] were the full rock band package:

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:41.560
handsome guys, long hair, vintage look,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:44.000
the soulful singer, the whole late '60s

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:48.160
rock sound, that vibe. But there was

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.840
just one small problem. The band did not

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.840
exist. It was all AI. The voices, the

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.040
music, the band members were all

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:01.920
generated using AI. But their music was

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:05.760
so familiar and so generic that it felt

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:09.119
like any other band. It was a perfectly

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.120
acceptable rock band, and that was

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.440
exactly why it worked. But, it is

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.240
completely generic. Everything looks and

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.600
feels like everything else. I call that

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.680
synthetic sameness. Now, synthetic

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:24.080
sameness makes the work undifferentiated

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.680
and inconsequential.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:30.160
Just another drop in the endless flood

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.920
of frictionless content, and that's the

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.080
paradox. AI can help you create more, it

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.560
can help you create faster, but it can

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.640
also make you completely

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.200
undifferentiated faster than anything

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.840
else. That's the very definition of

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:48.360
commodity. If everyone has access to

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:50.680
something for cheap or free, it stops

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.880
being an advantage. You know, I get

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:57.040
these two questions a lot. The first one

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.920
is, am I real or am I AI-generated? I am

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.120
not going to answer that question

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:05.040
because what if I said I was real, but I

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.000
was actually an AI that was trained to

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.400
say I was real?

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.239
What a mystery box.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:16.239
Uh just kidding. Of course, I am real.

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:18.800
The second question is about editing. Uh

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:21.519
I work with this awesome team from an

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.560
agency called Dragonfruit Media. They

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:26.080
were based in Los Angeles, now in New

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:29.560
York. Our editors and our creative

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.720
directors pour over every visual, every

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.080
detail.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.040
And the other day, we were talking about

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:37.600
the same topic, creativity and AI, and

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:40.840
they talked about this one tool they

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.400
love, Hixfield. It is an AI studio to

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.560
help you make stunning visuals, and this

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.880
video is sponsored by Hixfield, which

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.440
fits perfectly because that's the whole

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:53.120
point. Our team genuinely loves the

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:56.360
nexus of AI and creativity, and they

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.960
love using Higgs Field with Claude, but

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:02.080
the final choices and final decisions

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.440
are always made by humans. So, the

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.800
question of standing apart from the

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:10.480
crowd comes down to just one thing. Do

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.959
you know how to push AI far enough that

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.959
you can get something original out of

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:18.440
it? That is where we need the Edge

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:21.120
framework. The middle is a commodity.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:24.360
Edge is how you climb out of it. E

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.680
stands for exacting. Do you hold your

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:31.400
work to a higher standard? Do you edit

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:35.240
out everything that sounds generic and

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:37.400
cliché? D stands for differentiated.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:41.120
Does your work move away from the

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:44.120
obvious AI defaults? Do you bring your

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:47.840
own unique perspective, your own unique

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:51.240
point of view? G is grounded. Is it

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:54.520
rooted in concrete reality? Real-world

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.919
data? Is it verified? Or is it one of

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:59.200
those AI hallucinations that we're so

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:02.360
used to now? And the final E is

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.680
emotional. Does it make someone feel

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:07.160
something, something real?

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:10.360
Does it come from your heart, from your

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:13.760
human emotions? AI can't copy that. Now,

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:15.520
let's make it concrete. For example, in

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.600
one of my favorite movies, The Dark

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:21.240
Knight, Christopher Nolan, who is the

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:25.040
director of the movie, insisted on

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:28.960
flipping an actual massive truck in the

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.960
middle of the streets of Chicago.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:40.040
Now, it would be trivial to fake it

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:42.640
using CGI and computer effects. And

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:44.960
doing it in the real world would take so

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.160
much extra work. Then why do it? Because

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.080
Nolan wanted to have the same edge that

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.120
you and I want. In fact, he was applying

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.720
the same framework. Exacting standards,

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:59.320
differentiated approach, grounding it in

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:01.720
reality, and all of that created a real

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:04.120
emotional impact. And by the way, if you

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:06.400
like ideas like these and you want to go

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.880
deeper, you can subscribe to my

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.920
newsletter. It's every Tuesday. Link is

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.880
below.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.120
Totally free. So, let's walk through the

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:17.640
framework EDGE one at a time. Starting

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.400
with the first E, exacting standards.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:22.720
The most dangerous thing about AI

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:25.440
creativity is also the most useful thing

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:29.280
about it. AI never runs out of versions.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:33.240
If you ask a calculator, what is 8 * 7,

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:36.240
you get 56 every time, forever. But if

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:39.200
you ask an AI to write an opening scene

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.440
of a film that starts with a sunset over

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.080
an ocean, it might pick a different

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:46.680
scene every single time. Run it five

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:49.400
times, you get five different openings.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:53.000
Now, that's not a glitch. That is the

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:55.040
fundamental feature of AI. AI lives in

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:57.120
the world of probabilities. When you

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:00.280
send a prompt, here's what's happening

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.280
underneath. The AI breaks your words

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:05.320
into small pieces. They're called

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:08.240
tokens. And those tokens are then

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.880
converted into numbers and they pass

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.600
through this thing called a neural

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.520
network, which is just a bunch of

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.000
equations. And then it tries to

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.240
understand what you're asking. And when

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.840
AI responds to your prompt, it predicts

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:24.000
what's likely to come next, one word at

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:28.080
a time. So,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:31.640
if the line was once upon a, the AI most

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.120
likely will pick the word time. Once

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:37.360
upon a time. Now, lots of other words

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.520
would fit. Once upon a rooftop, once

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.200
upon a spreadsheet, once upon a parking

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:45.680
lot. But once upon a time is more likely

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:48.280
because AI has seen it more often and it

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:50.760
believes that the probability of that

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:53.040
word is higher because it's a very

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:56.040
well-known phrase. Now, if you ask a

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.880
random question, every time you ask, AI

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.520
is going to pick a different answer

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:03.120
because it's just picking from all

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.920
likely words that can fit and it's like

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.480
a constant roll of dice and that's why

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:10.720
it can surprise you. For the first time,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:14.320
there is a tool that doesn't just

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:17.520
execute your ideas, it can expand them

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:19.919
and take them in a direction that you

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:22.840
had never imagined before. To me, AI

00:08:19.919 --> 00:08:25.960
feels like this amazing jazz musician

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:27.480
who's playing in a swanky nightclub. You

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.680
don't know what you're going to get, but

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:33.280
here's the problem. A machine that

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.680
generates endlessly also hands you

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:39.159
endless noise. It's called AI slop

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.960
nowadays and that's why you need to be

00:08:39.159 --> 00:08:45.280
exacting because you have to be a

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:47.320
ruthless editor. 999

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:50.760
of those thousand options need to be

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:53.240
killed until you get that one thing that

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:55.880
sings to you and that is about your

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:59.720
judgment, about your taste. Here's an

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:02.120
example. A team made a 95-minute

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:05.000
AI-generated film that was shown at

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:08.040
around the Cannes Film Market just this

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:11.480
year in 2026. It was called Hell Grind.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:14.240
Now, it was made using the Hexfield

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.400
Seedance platform and the tools that

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.280
Hexfield gives you. It was a video made

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:22.160
in 4K, which is sort of high definition

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:25.640
and it was made by 15 people. It took

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:27.720
just 14 days and half a million dollars.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:31.120
But here's the thing, just to make the

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:34.120
first 25 minutes of that film, the team

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:36.560
generated over 16,000

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:39.800
clips, a machine-generated

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:41.640
mountain of material because that's what

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.960
AI is really good for.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:45.120
But only 253

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:48.480
of those

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.640
were selected. That's ruthless rejection

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:52.720
in action. Let's make it practical.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.480
Let's say you are a

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:58.640
small business owner who runs a coffee

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:00.400
shop and you want to create an ad. Now,

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:03.280
you can connect

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.040
creative tool like Hix Field to Claude

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:07.680
and you can set it up once, you can go

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.040
to settings, choose connectors, paste

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:13.240
the Hix Field link, and you're done in

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:15.640
about 30 seconds. After that, you just

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:18.160
talk to Claude in plain language, just

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:20.400
like you would prompt any AI, and Claude

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.960
will work with Hix Field for you. For

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:25.880
instance, you can ask Claude to have as

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:29.280
many variations of your ad as you want.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:31.280
One is in the warm morning light,

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:35.720
another is like a

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:38.640
a grainy nostalgic memory, another has a

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:41.160
sleek premium feel to it, and yet

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.720
another focused on happy customers, you

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.800
know, laughing, having a good time in

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.160
your coffee shop. You can have as many

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:50.200
variations as you want. Now, 10 years

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:53.520
ago, each one of these variations would

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:55.800
require a crew that comes into your shop

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:57.880
and shoots the footage and they go back

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:00.080
into the editing room for a week and it

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:03.240
would take you thousands and thousands

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.560
of dollars. Today, AI can give you 10

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.040
different directions in one afternoon.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:10.680
That's an incredible gift. A person with

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:12.839
an idea and a very small budget can now

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:14.839
compete with the largest agencies and

00:11:12.839 --> 00:11:17.240
studios in the world. Now, we go to the

00:11:14.839 --> 00:11:20.040
next one, differentiation. If you're

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:22.839
using the same AI the same way everybody

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:25.960
else does, how will you stand out? It's

00:11:22.839 --> 00:11:29.920
not AI's job to make you look different.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:31.920
It's yours. During the 2025 NBA finals,

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:34.480
there is a prediction marketing company

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:37.120
called Kalshi and they needed to make an

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:39.880
ad for national TV. Now, they went to

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:42.560
real studios and the studios quoted them

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:45.000
six and seven-figure budgets and months

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:48.200
and months of work. So, instead they

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.520
handed it to one person with AI skills

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:52.200
and told them

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:55.120
they didn't want anything normal. So, he

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:58.480
built something completely unhinged. An

00:11:55.120 --> 00:12:01.160
alien drinking beer, someone swimming in

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:04.280
eggs, and a cowboy holding a Chihuahua.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:08.400
The company generated 400 trials to then

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:12.080
find 15 usable shots. But, in about 3

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:14.680
days and after roughly $2,000, Kashi had

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:17.080
an ad and they aired it during the

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:18.520
finals and reached more than 18 million

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:22.520
impressions.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:25.920
Now, Kashi could have made a safe bet.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:28.320
But, then nobody would have cared.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:31.440
That's the power of differentiation. So,

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:33.640
differentiation is the real divide now.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:37.080
AI makes creation easy, but the real

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:39.680
competence is in your point of view,

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:42.560
your ability to stand out. And that

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:44.480
can't come from AI. That has to come

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:46.839
from you. Let's make it practical again.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:51.480
Let's walk through an example. Say

00:12:46.839 --> 00:12:53.880
you're building an app to avoid burnout.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:55.280
Now, there are 10 others who are making

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:58.320
the same app, they're in the same

00:12:55.280 --> 00:13:01.520
market, and every pitch is going to open

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:04.160
with a scene on the beach. So, throw out

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:07.240
that beach and open on the different

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:09.920
feeling instead. Maybe a phone stuck at

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:12.240
1% glowing in the dark. The city is

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:14.560
dark, everyone else is still texting,

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:16.760
and then the battery runs out, it all

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:17.960
goes dark. Same story,

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:20.440
but now

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:22.839
the room is leaning in because you just

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:24.760
didn't say burnout is real, you showed

00:13:22.839 --> 00:13:27.280
them something. You made them feel

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:29.240
something in just 3 seconds. And by the

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:31.360
way, that visual was generated with

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:33.680
Hexfield as well, and it was just as

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.800
easy as writing a prompt. So, you can do

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:37.880
this, too. When everyone is making the

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:40.680
same point, what's the point of saying

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:42.400
it louder? Show it from an angle they

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:44.640
haven't seen before. Here's something

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:46.960
actionable. Before asking AI to create

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:49.240
anything that needs to stand out, ask it

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:52.320
three questions. One, what would the

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:55.400
most obvious version of this look like?

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:58.080
Number two, what are five angles that

00:13:55.400 --> 00:14:00.120
move away from that default? And number

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.520
three, which one of those five would

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:03.920
make people stop and take notice? And

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:06.720
why? That's how you create

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:09.880
differentiation. AI can make the obvious

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:12.840
version beautiful. Differentiation is

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:15.560
finding the angle that others won't.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:18.040
Now, let's go to the next step, G for

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:20.720
grounded. This is where AI's biggest

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:23.240
weakness lives. AI makes things up and

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:25.160
is always confident about it.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:28.960
The other day,

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:31.600
I was using Gemini, and I pasted a

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:34.320
research screenshot and asked it to

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:36.920
analyze the data. Now, it gave me some

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:39.280
insights, but as I was interacting with

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:41.480
it, something felt

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:44.200
off. So,

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:45.880
I asked a simple follow-up question, and

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:48.480
I said, "Were you

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:51.520
actually able to read the data in the

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:54.800
screenshot?" And it said, basically,

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:58.240
"Sorry, I don't have the ability to read

00:14:54.800 --> 00:15:00.120
the data." And then I asked, "Okay, so

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.480
how did you come up with all those

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.920
insights?"

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:06.240
And it said, "I'm sorry, I got ahead of

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:07.040
myself and I completely fabricated

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:08.200
them."

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.839
Now,

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:13.800
here's the irony.

00:15:09.839 --> 00:15:15.760
Of course, I know that Gemini does have

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.280
the ability to read visual information

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:20.360
from a screenshot.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:22.960
But suddenly, when challenged, it was

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:27.400
not just hallucinating facts, it was

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:29.000
also confused about its own ability.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.920
So, it was like layers and layers of

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.880
hallucination.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:35.840
And I've seen the same thing with

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:39.160
ChatGPT, too. That is what we forget

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:42.360
often about AI. We start treating it

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:45.800
like some intelligent machine, some kind

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:49.760
of a confident expert. But sometimes AI

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:52.720
can slip and fall just like a toddler.

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:55.280
That is why grounding matters, because

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:58.280
AI will often create work that feels

00:15:55.280 --> 00:16:00.960
weightless, but smooth and fluent and

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:03.280
impressive, but it's not grounded.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:05.040
Nolan could have faked that truck flip

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:07.200
in The Dark Knight, too, right? And if

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:09.520
you use AI to do the same thing right

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:12.800
now, this is how it might look. But

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:16.360
instead, the team executed a practical

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:18.160
stunt with a giant truck on LaSalle

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:20.320
Street in Chicago.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:23.560
And that sequence works because when you

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:26.240
watch it, you feel the physical weight

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:29.280
of the real thing in the theater. The

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:32.839
street is real, the truck is real, the

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:36.120
danger feels real. It's all grounded in

00:16:32.839 --> 00:16:39.839
reality. So, that's the actionable move.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:42.360
Never let AI have the last word on

00:16:39.839 --> 00:16:45.000
anything that has to be true. You can do

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:47.680
three moves to ground it. First, make it

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:50.520
show the sources. Ask for the real

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:53.040
links, the real numbers, the real data,

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:55.480
the real quotes behind it. If it cannot

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:57.720
produce one, then that claim is a

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:59.720
fiction. The second move is to feed one

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:01.839
AI engine to the other, and I do it all

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:05.240
the time. I constantly ask Claude to

00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:07.720
verify ChatGPT's work, and ask ChatGPT

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:10.079
to verify Gemini's work. And so on. And

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:13.199
third, use apps like Google's Notebook

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:16.000
LM and feed it the data that's your own.

00:17:13.199 --> 00:17:19.160
That's what grounds AI. Like that giant

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:21.560
truck in Nolan's movie, the weight has

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:25.240
to feel real. And now, final E,

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:28.480
emotional. The same object can evoke

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:31.320
different emotions depending on context.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:34.480
Think about a candle. On a birthday

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:37.360
cake, it's all about joy. It's about

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:40.120
celebration. At a church, it's a prayer.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:42.760
During a blackout, it's family fun time.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:45.600
But at a vigil, it's about grief. And on

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:48.440
a date night, it's about romance and

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:51.240
intimacy. Same flame, but it burns a

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:54.120
completely different emotion every time.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:56.760
That's the one thing AI cannot do for

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:59.520
us. It can generate an image of a

00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:02.200
phenomenal candle and give it the the

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:04.240
the situational context, even put

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:07.080
appropriate music to it, but what

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:09.480
emotions will get lit in you when you

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:13.240
see that? Is entirely up to you.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:16.280
Coca-Cola remade their famous holiday ad

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:19.120
in 2024, and they used AI. And it had

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:22.280
the same feel, the same trucks, the

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:25.200
snow, the holiday imagery, the same

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:28.120
brand nostalgia. And every frame was

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:31.240
polished. And yet, many people called it

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:33.360
soulless. The images were familiar, but

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:36.960
somehow the emotions

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:39.760
didn't land. Now, they fixed it in 2025.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:41.120
AI knows how to give you the right

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:43.840
images,

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:46.400
but only you know what the spirit of

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:48.320
Christmas actually feels like. And

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:50.760
that's the ultimate takeaway [music]

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:54.280
from this whole video.

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:57.240
AI today can make anything you want.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:19:00.720
It makes movies, it makes music, it

00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:01.920
makes ads, pictures, lessons, whatever

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:04.880
you want.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:09.080
But every time something becomes

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:11.200
abundant, something else becomes scarce.

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:13.680
The parts that come from you [music]

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:18.920
having lived your life,

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:22.440
your memories, your wins, your losses,

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:24.560
the way you think, the way you feel.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:28.840
That's what makes you creative. In the

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:32.600
world of AI, that's your edge. You.

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:35.920
The machine can paint the candle flame.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:39.560
Only you can make it mean something.

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:41.400
Your creation isn't done when it looks

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:45.440
right.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:46.520
It's done when it feels right.

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:49.200
Thank you.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.200
And I love you.

